bone-tendon transitional-stiffness system and its disruption by anabolic steroids) got its
own entry, [[claim--tendon-insertion-transitional-stiffness-gradient-disrupted-by-steroids]].
Chapters 4-9 remain pending for later sessions; resume at Chapter 4, The Means of Special
Strength Training (printed 201, PDF idx 212)._

_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.1 "The Problem of Training Means" (4.1.1
Characteristics of Strength Increase, 4.1.2 The Effect of the Strength Training Means;
printed 201-206, PDF idx 212-217) is done, 6 entries. The short unnumbered lead-in before
4.1 (page 201/PDF 212, history-of-the-field narrative) produced no entry of its own; it is
scene-setting with no discrete claim. One dedup note: the 4.1.1 passage at printed 202
(PDF 213) repeats, via the same Verkhoshansky 1977 citation chain, the
Korobkov/Gerasimov/Vasiliev "strength gains are roughly uniform at 20/40/60/80% of 1RM in
early training" finding and the Hettinger & Muller (1953) one-third-max threshold finding
already captured in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] (Ch1, 1.2); that
identical material was NOT re-atomized. The genuinely new adjacent material in 4.1.1
(Zimkin 1961 and Muller & Hettinger 1957 training-age-dependent load percentages;
Chernyavsky 1966 light/heavy load-proportion result) became
[[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]]. Two further new
claim entries cover delayed onset of strength gains
([[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]) and detraining/
retention timelines, including an unreconciled internal contradiction between the
Chernyavsky 20-session decay curve and the Vasiliev/McMorris & Eklins 40-session retention
data ([[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]). Section 4.1.2's "Algebraic
Relations" passage (Commutative/Associative Laws applied to training order) and its
Acute/Delayed Training Effect and Particular/Cumulative Effect taxonomy became two framework
entries ([[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]],
[[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]); the remaining
"Steps Towards Structuring the Training Process" material (training load's five
characteristics; volume's two functions; structured system beats random combination even at
lower volume, citing Verkhoshansky 1966/1970, Tatyan 1974, Khodykin 1975; Figure 4.1,
verified against the page image) became
[[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]. Chapter
4 sections 4.2 (Different Means of Strength Development: electrostimulation, resistance and
kinetic-energy training, isometric, eccentric, isokinetic, machines, symmetric training,
muscle isolation), 4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General
Endurance) remain pending; resume at 4.2, printed 207 / PDF idx 218, "4.2 Different Means of
Strength Development" / "4.2.1 Electrostimulation."_

